##Assumptions & Known Limitations
    #System Assumptions
        Deterministic Inputs: Matrix elements are seeded deterministically to guarantee reproducible workload complexity across runs.

    #Invertibility: 
        Generated matrices are strictly diagonally dominant, guaranteeing pivot non-zero integrity during Gauss-Jordan elimination.

    #Environment: 
        Executed in a POSIX-compliant environment with CLOCK_MONOTONIC support.

##Known Limitations
    # 1 - Mutex Contention: 
        A single global mutex protects the bounded queue. At high worker counts (N > 8) or with trivial job workloads, queue synchronization overhead can become a performance bottleneck.
    # 2 - Hardware Constraints: 
        Parallel scaling speedup S(N) = T(1) / T(N) is bounded by physical CPU core count and Intel P-core/E-core scheduling asymmetry under WSL 2.

